  "text": "```verilog\nmodule isochronous_4phase_handshake (\n    input  src_clk_i,\n    input  src_rst_ni,\n    input  src_valid_i,\n    output src_ready_o,\n    input  dst_clk_i,\n    input  dst_rst_ni,\n    output dst_valid_o,\n    input  dst_ready_i\n);\n\n    // Synchronizer for src_valid_i into dst domain\n    reg [1:0] src_valid_sync;\n    always @(posedge dst_clk_i or negedge dst_rst_ni) begin\n        if (!dst_rst_ni)\n            src_valid_sync <= 2'b00;\n        else\n            src_valid_sync <= {src_valid_sync[0], src_valid_i};\n    end\n    wire src_valid_dst = src_valid_sync[1];\n\n    // Synchronizer for dst_ready_i into src domain\n    reg [1:0] dst_ready_sync;\n    always @(posedge src_clk_i or negedge src_rst_ni) begin\n        if (!src_rst_ni)\n            dst_ready_sync <= 2'b00;\n        else\n            dst_ready_sync <= {dst_ready_sync[0], dst_ready_i};\n    end\n    wire dst_ready_src = dst_ready_sync[1];\n\n    // Synchronizer for src_ready_o into dst domain\n    reg [1:0] src_ready_sync;\n    always @(posedge dst_clk_i or negedge dst_rst_ni) begin\n        if (!dst_rst_ni)\n            src_ready_sync <= 2'b00;\n        else\n            src_ready_sync <= {src_ready_sync[0], src_ready_o};\n    end\n    wire src_ready_dst = src_ready_sync[1];\n\n    // Synchronizer for dst_valid_o into src domain\n    reg [1:0] dst_valid_sync;\n    always @(posedge src_clk_i or negedge src_rst_ni) begin\n        if (!src_rst_ni)\n            dst_valid_sync <= 2'b00;\n        else\n            dst_valid_sync <= {dst_valid_sync[0], dst_valid_o};\n    end\n    wire dst_valid_src = dst_valid_sync[1];\n\n    // Source side: generate src_ready_o\n    // src_ready_o should be high when the source is ready to accept a new transfer.\n    // In a four-phase handshake, the source asserts valid, waits for ready, then deasserts valid.\n    // The source is ready when it is not in the middle of a transfer.\n    // We track the state of the source side.\n    \n    // Let's think about the four-phase handshake protocol:\n    // Phase 1: src_valid_i = 0, src_ready_o = 0 (idle)\n    // Phase 2: src_valid_i = 1, src_ready_o = 0 (source asserts valid)\n    // Phase 3: src_valid_i = 1, src_ready_o = 1 (destination asserts ready)\n    // Phase 4: src_valid_i = 0, src_ready_o = 1 (source deasserts valid)\n    // Then back to Phase 1: src_valid_i = 0, src_ready_o = 0\n    \n    // The source controls src_valid_i (input to this module) and src_ready_o (output).\n    // Wait, src_valid_i is an input, meaning the source side (outside this module) drives it.\n    // And src_ready_o is an output, meaning this module drives it back to the source.\n    // Similarly, dst_valid_o is an output (this module drives it to destination), and dst_ready_i is an input (destination drives it back).\n    \n    // So this module is the \"bridge\" between source and destination.\n    // The source drives src_valid_i and receives src_ready_o.\n    // The destination drives dst_ready_i and receives dst_valid_o.\n    \n    // For the four-phase handshake:\n    // Source side: source asserts src_valid_i, waits for src_ready_o to go high, then deasserts src_valid_i.\n    // Destination side: destination sees dst_valid_o, asserts dst_ready_i, waits for dst_valid_o to go low, then deasserts dst_ready_i.\n    \n    // This module needs to:\n    // 1. When src_valid_i is asserted (and synchronized to dst domain), assert dst_valid_o.\n    // 2. When dst_ready_i is asserted (and synchronized to src domain), assert src_ready_o.\n    // 3. When src_ready_o is high and src_valid_i is high, the transfer is complete. The source will deassert src_valid_i.\n    // 4. When dst_valid_o is low and dst_ready_i is high, the destination will deassert dst_ready_i.\n    \n    // Let's use a state machine in each domain.\n    \n    // Source domain state machine for src_ready_o\n    // States: IDLE (ready=0), WAIT_VALID (ready=0, waiting for valid), WAIT_DEASSERT (ready=1, waiting for valid to deassert)\n    // Actually, let's think more carefully.\n    \n    // src_ready_o should be:\n    // - 0 when no transfer is in progress and we're not expecting a valid\n    // - 0 when valid is asserted but we haven't confirmed the transfer yet\n    // - 1 when the transfer is confirmed (dst has accepted) and we're waiting for valid to deassert\n    // - 0 when valid has deasserted (back to idle)\n    \n    // Let's use a simple approach:\n    // src_ready_o = dst_ready_src (synchronized)\n    // But we need to ensure that src_ready_o only goes high when a valid transfer is being completed.\n    \n    // Actually, in a four-phase handshake, the ready signal is generated by the receiver.\n    // The source asserts valid, the destination asserts ready when it's ready to accept.\n    // When both valid and ready are high, the transfer occurs.\n    // Then valid deasserts, then ready deasserts.\n    \n    // So:\n    // src_ready_o should be high when the destination is ready to accept a transfer.\n    // dst_valid_o should be high when the source has a valid transfer.\n    \n    // Let's define:\n    // dst_valid_o = src_valid_dst (synchronized src_valid_i)\n    // src_ready_o = dst_ready_src (synchronized dst_ready_i)\n    \n    // But we need to be careful about the four-phase protocol. The ready should only be asserted when there's a valid, or the valid should only be asserted when there's a ready? No, in four-phase, they can be independent.\n    \n    // Let's just do:\n    assign dst_valid_o = src_valid_dst;\n    assign src_ready_o = dst_ready_src;\n\nendmodule\n```<|im_end|>",
